     ssoorrtt reads lines from each _f_i_l_e, or from the standard input
     if none is given, sorts them, and writes the result to the
     standard output.  By default the sort key is the entire line
     and lines are compared byte for byte.

     ssoorrtt does its work with a polyphase merge: it first reads
     the input into temporary files, using replacement selection
     to build large initial runs, and then repeatedly merges
     those runs together in the temporary files until one run
     remains, which becomes the output.

     A key field is selected by one or two _p_o_s_i_t_i_o_n arguments,
     +_m._n_f and -_m._n_f.  _m is the number of fields to skip and _n
     the number of characters to skip within the following field
     before the key starts (for -mm.nnff, before it ends); fields
     are separated by blanks and tabs, or by the character set
     with -tt, and leading separators before a field are also
     skipped once _m is nonzero.  _f may contain any of the key-or-
     dering letters bb dd ff ii nn rr listed below; these apply only to
     this one key, overriding the global ordering options for it.
     If no position is given, the key is the whole line.  More
     than one +_m._n_f [-_m._n_f] pair may be given, and are applied in
     order until one pair compares unequal.

